  "query_preview": "..Considerable cloud cover over the area as of 3 am\nwith scattered showers and thunderstorms, some of which have\nbeen rather efficient producing heavy rain with PWATs between \n1.30\" and 2\". Confidence low with forecast evolution as the day\nprogresses and the extent of flash flooding as any storms will \nbe efficient rain makers. Current cloud cover, morning \nshowers/storms and possible MCV may delay the start of afternoon\nconvection and push it more toward this evening. Another factor\nis possible weak upper level disturbance, that was over \nsouthern Sonora MX last night, moving across the area under \nsouthern flow as it shifts today to become more southwesterly. \nPer conference call earlier this AM with WPC and the offices \nfrom AZ/NM/UT/NV the SLGT risk for Excessive Rainfall was \nexpanded at across most of SE AZ and the state. Due to my low \nconfidence about how today will evolve, all models don't have \nthe answer and they muddy the waters, I decided against hoisting\nFlash Flood Watch\n\nTonight: As the southwesterly flow aloft increases as an upper \nlevel trof moves into the west coast, there is the potential for\nanother overnight period of showers and thunderstorms, with the\nfocus being across Pinal, Graham and Greenlee counties.\n\nFriday into this weekend: A downturn in daily thunderstorm is\nexpected as drier air pushes across the area under southwest \nflow aloft. Isolated thunderstorms at best, and mostly east and\nsouth of Tucson of Tucson. \n\nEarly next week: Possible slight uptick in thunderstorms\nassociated with easterly wave passing by across northern MX.\nConfidence low.\n\nTemperatures will be about 3 to 6 degrees below normal today\nand Thursday, with warming thereafter to above normal values \nfor this weekend.",
